G3521.d:
νηστεία, -ας, ἡ
 (νηστεύω), [in LXX for צוֹם ;] 
fasting, a fast; 
  (a) of voluntary abstinence from food: Matt.17:21 (WH, R, txt., om.), Mark.9:29 (WH, txt., R, txt., om.), Luke.2:37, Acts.14:23; of the Day of Atonement, Acts.27:9; 
  (b) of involuntary abstinence: 2 Cor.6:5 11:27.†
 SYN.: ἀσιτία, q.v  (AS)

G3581.d:
ξένος, -η, -ον 
 [in LXX chiefly for נׇכְרִי ;] 
  (a) foreign, alien: δαιμόνια, Acts.17:18; διδαχαί Heb.13:9; 
  (b) with genitive of thing(s), strange to, estranged from, ignorant of: Eph.2:12; 
  (with) strange, unusual: 1 Pet.4:12. 
 As subst., ὁ ξ., 
  (a) a foreigner, stranger: Matt.25:35, 38 25:43-44 27:7, Acts.17:21, 3 John.5; ξέίνοι κ. τάροικοι (opposite to συμπολῖται, οίκεῖοι), Eph.2:19; ξ. καὶ παρεπίδημοι,  Heb.11:13; 
  (b) one of the parties bound by ties of hospitality; 
  α the guest; 
  β the host (= ξενοδόκος, Hom., Ii, xv, 532) : Rom.16:23.†
 (AS)

G3606.d:
ὅθεν 
 adv., 
whence; 
  (a) of direction or source: Matt.12:44, Luke.11:24, Acts.14:26 28:13; = ἐκεῖθεν ὅπου, Matt.25:24, 26 (cf. Thuc., i, 89, 3); ὅ. γινώσκομεν, 1 John.2:18; 
  (b) of cause, whence, wherefore: Matt.14:7, Acts.26:19, Heb.2:17 3:1 7:25 8:3 9:18 11:19.†
 (AS)

G3625.d:
οἰκουμένη, -ης, ἡ
 (fern. Pres. pass. ptcp. of οἰκέω; sc. γῆ) [in LXX chiefly for תֵּבֵל, אֶרֶץ ;] 
the inhabited earth; 
  (a) in cl., the countries occupied by Greeks, as disting. from barbarian lands (Hdt., Dem., al.); 
  (b) in later writers, the Roman world: Luke.2:1, Acts.11:28 24:5; by meton., of its inhabitants : Acts.17:6 19:27; 
  (with) in LXX (Ps.23:1 71:8, al.) and NT, also of the whole inhabited world: Matt.24:14, Luke.4:5 21:26, Rom.10:18, Heb.1:6, Rev.3:10 16:14; by meton. (ut supr.), Acts.17:31, Rev.12:9; 
  (d) of the Messianic age, ἡ οἰ. ἡ μέλλουσα = ὁ αἰὼ ὁ μέλλων: Heb.2:5.†
 (AS)

G3650.d:
ὅλος, -η, -ον, 
 [in LXX chiefly for כֹּל ;] 
of persons and things, whole, entire, complete; 
  1. of indefinite ideas, with subst. anarth.: Luke.5:5, Acts.11:26 28:30, Titus.1:11; ὅλον ἄνθρωπον (an entire man; see Field, Notes, 93), John.7:23; ὅλη Ἰερουσαλήμ (= πᾶσα Ἰ, Matt.2:3; see Bl., §47, 9), Acts.21:31. 
  2. Definite, with art.; 
  (a) preceding subst.: Matt.4:23, 24 Luke.8:39, 1 Cor.12:17, al.; 
  (b) following subst.: Mark.1:33, Luke.9:25, John.4:53, Acts.21:30, al.; 
  (with) between art. and subst., where subst. is an abstract noun (Plat., al.). 
  3. Attached to adj. or verb: Matt.13:33, Luke.13:21, John.9:34, al.; adverbially, δι ̓ ὅλου (MM, xviii), John.19:23. 
 (AS)

G3667.d:
ὁμοίωμα, -τος, τό
 (ὁμοιόω), [in LXX for דְּמוּת, תַּבְנִית, תְּמוּנָה, etc. ;] 
that which is made like something; 
  (a) concrete, an image, like­ness (Ps.106:20, Eze.1:5, 1Ma.3:48, al.) : Rev.9:7; 
  (b) abstract, like­ness, resemblance: Rom.5:14 6:5 8:3, Phil.2:7; ἐν ὁ. εἰκόνος, Rom.1:23.†
 SYN.: εἰκών (which see), ὁμοίωσις  (AS)

G3674.d:
ὁμοῦ
  adv. (ὁμός), 
together; 
  (a) prop., of place: John.21:2, Acts.2:1; 
  (b) without idea of place: John.4:36 20:4.†
 (AS)

G3705.d:
ὅραμα, -τος, τό
 (ὁράω), [in LXX for חָזוֹן, מַרְאֶה, etc. ;] 
that which is seen; 
  (a) a sight, spectacle: Matt.17:9, Acts.7:31; 
  (b) an appearance, vision: Acts.9:10, 12 (Rec.) Acts.10:3, 17 10:19 11:5 12:9 16:9-10 18:9 (cf. ὀπτασία).†
 (AS)

G3709.d:
ὀργή, -ῆς, ἡ 
 [in LXX chiefly for אַף, also for חָרוֹן, חֵמָה, קָצַף, etc. ;] 
  1 1, impulse, propensity, disposition. 
  2. anger, wrath; 
  (a) of men: Mark.3:5, Eph.4:31, Col.3:8, 1 Tim.2:8, Jas.1:19-20; 
  (b) of God; 
  α that reaction of the divine nature against sin which in anthropomorphic language is called anger: Rom.1:18 9:22 12:19, (ICC, in l), 1 Thess.1:10 2:16, Heb.3:11 4:3" (LXX) Rev.14:10 16:19 19:15; 
  β of the effect of God's anger: Matt.3:7, Luke.3:7 21:23, John.3:36, Rom.2:5, 8 3:5 4:15 5:9 13:4-5 Eph.5:6, Col.3:6, 1 Thess.5:9, Jas.1:20, Rev.6:16-17 11:18; σκεύη ὀργῆς, Rom.9:22; τέκνα ὀργῆς, Eph.2:3.†
 SYN.: see: θυμός  (AS)

G3717.d:
ὀρθός, -ή, -όν 
 [in LXX chiefly for יָשָׁר ;] 
straight; 
  (a) in height, straight, upright: Acts.14:10; 
  (b) in line, straight, direct: figuratively, Heb.12:13 (LXX).†
 (AS)

G3745.d:
ὅσος, -η, -ον, 
 correlat. of τοσοῦτος,  
how much, how many, how great, how far, how long, as much as, etc. (= Lat. quantus); 
  (a) of number and quantity: m. pl., Matt.14:36, Mark.3:10, Rom.2:12, al.; n. pl., Matt.17:12, Mark.10:21, Luke.11:8, al.; πάτνες (πάντα) ὅ., Matt.13:46, Luke.4:40, al.; before οὗτοι (ταῦτα), Rom.8:14, Phil.4:8; with indic., Mark.6:56, Rev.3:19; with subjc. Mark.3:28, al.; ὅ. ἄν, Matt.18:18, John.11:22, al.; 
  (b) of measure and degree: Mark.3:8, Luke.8:39, Acts.9:13; in compar. sent., ὅσον before μᾶλλον, Mark.7:36; καθ᾽ ὅσον, with compar.. Heb.3:3; before τοσοῦτο, Heb.7:20; οὕτως, Heb.9:27; τοσαύτῳ, with compar. before ὅσῳ, with compar., Heb.1:4; ἐφ᾽ ὅσον, inasmuch as, Matt.25:40, 45 Rom.11:13; 
  (with) of space and time: Rev.21:16; ἐφ᾽ ὅσον, as long as, Matt.9:15, 2 Pet.1:13; ἐφ᾽ ὅ. χρόνον, Rom.7:1, 1 Cor.7:39, Gal.4:1; ἔτι μικρὸν ὅσον ὅσον, yet how very short a time, Heb.10:37 (LXX). 
 (AS)

G3748.d:
ὅστις, ἥτις, ὅ τι (also written ὅ, τι and ὅτι; see LS, see word; WH, §411; Tdf., Pr., 111), in NT scarcely ever except in nom. (M, Pr., 91), the only instance of the oblique cases being found in ἕως ὅτου (see: ἕως), relative of indef. reference (related to simple ὅς as Lat. quisquis to qui), 
 whoever, anyone who; 
  (a) of an indef. person or thing: in general statements, Matt.5:39, 41 13:12, and freq., Luke.14:27, Gal.5:4, al.; in relative sentences, Matt.7:26, Luke.15:7, Phil.3:7, al.; πᾶς, with indic., Matt.7:24 10:32; ὅ ἄν (ἐάν), with subjc., Matt.12:50 13:12, John.14:13, 1 Cor.16:2, Gal.5:10, al.; 
  (b) of a definite person or thing, indicating quality, "either  generic, which, as other like things, or essential, which by its very nature" (Hort on 1 Pet.2:11), who is such as: Matt.2:6 7:26, Luke.2:10 7:37, John.8:53, Acts.7:53, Rom.6:2, 1 Cor.3:17, Gal.4:24, Eph.1:23, al.; 
  (with) where the relative sentence expresses a reason, consequence, etc. (M, Pr., 92), seeing that he (it, they), and he (it, they): Luke.8:3 10:42, Acts.10:47 11:28, Phil.4:3, al.; 
  (d) as in Ionic and late Greek (Bl., §50, 1; M, Pr., l.with), differing but little from ὅς: Luke.2:4 9:30, Acts.17:10, Rev.12:13. 
 (AS)

G3752.d:
ὅταν (for ὅτ᾽ ἄν = ὅτε ἄν), 
 temporal particle, 
with a conditional sense, usually of things expected to occur in an indefinite future; 
  1. prop., whenever; 
  (a) (a) with subjc. praes.: Matt.6:2, 5 Mark.14:7, Luke.11:36, John.7:27, Acts.23:35, 1 Cor.3:4, al.; ἕως τ. ἡμέρας ἐκείνης, ὅ., Matt.26:29, Mark.14:25; before τότε, 1 Thess.5:3; 
  (b) with subjc. aor. (M, Pr., 185): Matt.5:11, Mark.4:15, Luke.6:22, John.2:10 8:28 (Field, Notes, 94), 10:4, al. 
  2. As in Hom. (LS, see word), but not in cl. prose, with indic., when (M, Pr., 167f.; Bl., §65, 9): with impf., Mark.3:11(cf. Gen.38:9, al.); with praes., Mark.11:25; with fut.. Rev.4:9 (Swete, in l.); with aor., Mark.11:19, Rev.8:1 (Swete, in ll.; M, Pr., 168, 248; Field, Notes, 35). 
 (AS)

G3755.d:
ὅστις, ἥτις, ὅ τι (also written ὅ, τι and ὅτι; see LS, see word; WH, §411; Tdf., Pr., 111), in NT scarcely ever except in nom. (M, Pr., 91), the only instance of the oblique cases being found in ἕως ὅτου (see: ἕως), relative of indef. reference (related to simple ὅς as Lat. quisquis to qui), 
 whoever, anyone who; 
  (a) of an indef. person or thing: in general statements, Matt.5:39, 41 13:12, and freq., Luke.14:27, Gal.5:4, al.; in relative sentences, Matt.7:26, Luke.15:7, Phil.3:7, al.; πᾶς, with indic., Matt.7:24 10:32; ὅ ἄν (ἐάν), with subjc., Matt.12:50 13:12, John.14:13, 1 Cor.16:2, Gal.5:10, al.; 
  (b) of a definite person or thing, indicating quality, "either  generic, which, as other like things, or essential, which by its very nature" (Hort on 1 Pet.2:11), who is such as: Matt.2:6 7:26, Luke.2:10 7:37, John.8:53, Acts.7:53, Rom.6:2, 1 Cor.3:17, Gal.4:24, Eph.1:23, al.; 
  (with) where the relative sentence expresses a reason, consequence, etc. (M, Pr., 92), seeing that he (it, they), and he (it, they): Luke.8:3 10:42, Acts.10:47 11:28, Phil.4:3, al.; 
  (d) as in Ionic and late Greek (Bl., §50, 1; M, Pr., l.with), differing but little from ὅς: Luke.2:4 9:30, Acts.17:10, Rev.12:13. 
 (AS)

G3757.d:
oὗ
 (prop. genitive of ὅς), 
adv. of place, where, whither; 
  (a) in answer to the question "where?" (= ubi): Matt.2:9 18:20, Luke.4:16-17 23:53, Acts.1:13 2:2 7:29 12:12 16:13 20:6, 8 25:10 28:14, Rom.9:26" (LXX), Col.3:1, Heb.3:9" (LXX), Rev.17:15; of condition, Rom.4:15 5:20, 2 Cor.3:17; 
  (b) in answer to the question "whither?" (= quo): Matt.28:16, Luke.10:1 24:28; before ἐάν, with subjc., 1 Cor.16:6.†
 (AS)

G3778.d:
οὗτος, αὕτη, τοῦτο, genitive, τούτου, ταύτης, τούτου, 
 [in LXX chiefly for זֹאת ,זֶה ;] 
 demonstr. pron. (related to ἐκεῖνος as hic to ille),  
this; 
  1. as subst., this one, he; 
  (a) absol.: Matt.3:17, Mark.9:7, Luke.7:44, 45, John.1:15, Acts.2:15, al.; expressing contempt (cl.), Matt.13:55, 56, Mark.6:2, 3, John.6:42, al.; εἰς τοῦτο, Mark.1:38, Rom.14:9; μετὰ τοῦτο (ταῦτα; V. Westc. on John.5:1), John.2:12 11:7, al.; 
  (b) epanaleptic (referring to what precedes): Matt.5:19, Mark.3:35, Luke.9:48, John.6:46, Rom.7:10, al.; 
  (with) proleptic (referring to what follows): before ἵνα (Bl., §69, 6), Luke.1:43, John.3:19 (and freq.) 15:8, Rom.14:9, al.; before ὅτι, Luke.10:11, John.9:3o, Acts.24:14, Rom.2:3, al.; ὅπως, Rom.9:17; ἐάν, John.13:35; 
  (d) special idioms: τοῦτο μὲν . . . τ. δέ (cl), partly . . . partly. Heb.10:33; καὶ τοῦτο (τοῦτον, ταῦτα), and that (him) too, Rom.13:11, 1 Cor.2:2, Heb.11:12; τοῦτ᾽ ἐστιν, Matt.27:46.
  2. As adj., with subst.; 
  (a) with art. 
  (α) before the art.: Matt.12:32, Mark.9:29, Luke.7:44, John.4:15, Rom.11:24, Rev.19:9, al.; 
  (β) after the noun: Matt.3:9, Mark.12:16, Luke.11:31, John.4:13, Acts.6:13, Rom.15:28, 1 Cor.1:20, Rev.2:24, al.; 
  (b) with subst. anarth. (with predicative force; Bl., §49, 4): Luke.1:36 2:2 24:21, John.2:11 4:54 21:14, 2 Cor.13:1.
 (AS)

G3780.d:
οὐχί 
 strengthened form of οὐ, 
 not; 
  (a) in neg. sentences, not, not at all: Luke.1:60 12:51, John.13:10, al.; 
  (b) more frequently in questions where an affirm. ans. is expected (Lat. nonne) : Matt.5:46-47, Luke.6:39, John.11:9, al. 
 (AS)

G3788.d:
ὀφθαλμός, -οῦ, ὁ, 
 [in LXX chiefly for עַיִן ;] 
the eye (as in cl., chiefly pl.): Matt.5:38, Mark.9:47, Luke.6:41, John.9:6, al.; τοὺς ὀ. ἐξορύθσσειν (figuratively), Gal.4:15; ἐπᾶραι, Luke.6:20, John.6:5; ἀνοῖξα, Acts.9:40; id., of restoring sight, Matt.20:33, John.9:10, al.; ἐν ῥιπῇ ὀφθαλμοῦ, 1 Cor.15:52; by anthropom., of  God, Heb.4:13, 1 Pet.3:12; pleonastically (cf. Thackeray, Gr., 42f.), εἶδον οἱ ὀ. μοθ, Luke.2:30 (similarly, ib. 4:20 10:23, John.12:40, 1 Cor.2:9, 1 John.1:1, Rev.1:7). Metaphorical (as otherwise in cl.; see LS, see word); 
  (a) of ethical qualities: ὀ. πονηρός (meton., for envy; cf. Heb. עַיִן רַע, Pro.28:22; cf. Sir.14:10 34:13), Matt.6:22, 23 Mark.7:22, Luke.11:34; ἁπλοῦς, Matt.6:22, Luke.11:34; ἐπιθυμία (which see) ὀφθαλμῶν (cf. Ecc.4:8, Sir.14:9), 1 John.2:16; ὀ. μεστοὶ μοιχαλἰδος, 2 Pet.2:14; 
  (b) of mental vision: Matt.13:15, Mark.8:18, Luke.19:42, John.12:40, Rom.11:8, Gal.3:1, Eph.1:18, al.; ἐν ὀφθαλμοῖς before genitive (on the absence of the art., seeBl, §46, 9.II; M, Pr., 81), Matt.21:42, Mark.12:11.
 (AS)

G3824.d:
παλινγενεσία (Rec. παλιγγ-), -ας, ἡ 
 (πάλιν, γενεσις), 
new birth, renewal, restoration, regeneration; 
  (a) of persons (Plut., Phil., al.), of spiritual regeneration, διὰ λουτροῦ παλινγενεστίας (genitive attr., see Ellic., in l), Titus.3:5; 
  (b) of the world, as in Stoics, Jewish Apocal., al. (see Dalman, Words, 177 ff.), Matt.19:28.†
 (AS)

G3835.d:
πανοῦργος, -ον 
 [in LXX chiefly for עָרוּם ;] 
ready to do anything; 
  (a) in cl., chiefly in bad sense, knavish, crafty: 2 Cor.12:16; 
  (b) in good sense, skilful, clever (Pro.13:1 28:2).†
 (AS)

G3843.d:
πάντως 
 adv. (πάς), [in LXX: 4Ki.5:11 (see Thackeray, Gr., 47), Tob.14:8, al. ;] 
altogether, by all means; 
  (a) without neg. (from Hdt. on) : 1 Cor.16:12; esp. in strong affirmations, surely, at all events: Luke.4:23, Acts.21:22 28:4, 1 Cor.9:10; 
  (b) with neg. (so always in mom.), in a complete negation: Rom.3:9; in a partial negation (Bl., § 75, 7), 1 Cor.5:10.†
 (AS)

G3858.d:
παρα-δέχομαι 
 [in LXX: Exod.23:1 (נָשָׂא), Pro.3:12 (רָצָה), 2Ma.4:22 R, 3Ma.7:12 * ;] 
to receive, admit; 
  (a) of things: Mark.4:20, Acts.15:4 16:21 22:18, 1 Tim.5:19; 
  (b) of persons: Heb.12:6 (LXX).†
 (AS)

G3925.d:
παρ-εμ-βολή, -ῆς, ἡ
 (παρεμβάλλω), [in LXX chiefly for מַחֲנֶה ;] 
  1. an insertion, interpolation (Æschin.) 
  2. In the Macedonian dialect (Rutherford, NPhr., 473), as a military term; 
  (a) an army in battle array: Heb.11:34 (cf. Exod.14:19-20, Jdg.4:16, al.; frequently in Polyb.); 
  (b) a camp (Exod.29:14, al.) : Heb.13:11, 13 Rev.20:9; 
  (with) barracks, soldiers' quarters: Acts.21:34, 37 22:24 23:10, 16 23:32 28:16 (WH, txt., R, om.).†
 παρ-εμ-βάλλω, 
 [in LXX frequently and chiefly for מַחֲנֶה ;] 
  1. to put in beside or between, interpose. 
  2. In late writers (Polyb., al., LXX) as technical military term; 
  (a) of soldiers, to draw up in line (frequently in 1Ma.2:32, al.); 
  (b) of siege works, to cast up: with accusative and dative, χάρακά σοι, Luke.19:43.†
 (AS)

G3928.d:
παρ-ἐρχομαι 
 [in LXX chiefly for עָבַר ;] 
  1. to pass, pass by; 
  (a) of persons: absol., Luke.18:37; with accusative of person(s), Mark.6:48; with accusative lot., Acts.16:8; before διά, with genitive, Matt.8:28; 
  (b) of things : τ. ποτήριον, Matt.26:39 (ἀπ᾽ ἐμοῦ), Matt.26:42; of time, Matt.14:15, Mark.14:35, Acts.27:9, 1 Pet.4:3. Metaphorical, 
  (a) to pass away, perish: Matt.5:18 24:34-35 Mark.13:30-31, Luke.16:17 21:32-33, 2 Cor.5:17, Jas.1:10, 2 Pet.3:10; 
  (b) to pass by, neglect, disregard: with accusative of thing(s), Luke.11:42 15:29. 
  2. to come to, arrive: Luke.12:37 17:7, Acts.24:7, R, mg. (cf. ἀντι-παρέρχομαι).†
 SYN.: παραβαίνω  (AS)

G3958.d:
πάσχω 
 [in LXX: Amo.6:6 (חָלָה ni.), Wis.12:27, Sir.38:16, al. ;] 
to suffer, be acted on, as opposite to acting, often limited by a word expressive of good or evil; 
  (a) of misfortunes (most frequently without any limiting word) : absol., Luke.22:15 24:46, Acts.1:3 3:18 17:3, 1 Cor.12:26, Heb.2:18 9:26 13:12, 1 Pet.2:19-20 2:23 3:17 4:15 4:19; before ὑπό, with genitive, Matt.17:12; ὑπέρ, Acts.9:16, Phil.1:20, 2 Thess.1:5, 1 Pet.2:21; with dative ref., 1 Pet.4:1; περί, with genitive (before ὑπέρ), 1 Pet.3:18; διά, with accusative, 1 Pet.3:14; ὀλίγον (a little while), 1 Pet.5:10; with accusative, Matt.27:19, Mark.9:12, Luke.13:2 24:26, Acts.28:5, 2 Tim.1:12, Heb.5:8, Rev.2:10; παθήματα, 2 Cor.1:6; ταῦτα, Gal.3:4 (EV; cf. Lft., in l, but see infr.); accusative before ἀπό, Matt.16:21, Luke.9:22 17:25; ὑπό, Mark.5:26, 1 Thess.2:14; 
  (b) of pleasant experiences (but always with qualifying word, εὖ or accusative of thing(s)) : Gal.3:4 (cf. Grimm-Thayer, see word; Interp. Corn., in l, but see supr.) (cf. προ-, συν-πάσχω).†
 (AS)

G3962.d:
πατήρ, πατρός, -τρί, τέρα,  [in LXX chiefly and very frequently for אָב ;] 
a father; 
  1. prop., 
  (a) of the male parent: Matt.2:22, Luke.1:17, John.4:53; anarth., Heb.12:7 (M, Pr., 82 f.); pl., of both parents (cl.), Heb.11:23; οἱ π. τ. σαρκός, Heb.12:9; 
  (b) of a forefather or ancestor (in cl. usually in pl.; Hom., al.): Matt.3:9, Luke.1:73, John.8:39, al.; pl., Matt.23:30, 32 Luke.6:23, 26 John.4:20, 1 Cor.10:1, al.
  2. Metaphorical, 
  (a) of an author, originator, or archetype (= αἴτιος, ἀρχηγός, etc.; Pind., Plat., al.): John.8:41-44, Rom.4:11, 12 16. 
  (b) as a title of respect or honour, used of seniors, teachers and others in a position of responsible authority (Jdg.17:10, 2Ki.2:12, Pro.1:8, al.): Matt.23:9 Acts.7:2 22:1, 1 John.2:13. 
  3. Of God (as in cl. of Zeus) as Father; 
  (a) of created things: τ. φώτων, Jas.1:17; 
  (b) of all sentient beings: Eph.3:14, 15 Heb.12:9; 
  (with) of men, esp. those in covenant relation with Him (freq in OT and later Jewish lit.; see Dalman, Words, 184ff.): Matt.6:4, Luke.6:36, John.4:21, Jas.3:9, al.; ὁ π. ὁ ἐν (τ.) οὐρανοῖς, Matt.5:16, Mark.11:25; ὁ π. ὁ οὐράνιος, Matt.6:14 15:13; esp. in the Epp., of Christians: Rom.8:15, 2 Cor.6:18, Gal.4:6, Eph.2:18 4:6, 1 John.2:1; with genitive qual., τ. οἰκτιρμῶν, 2 Cor.1:3; τ. δοξῆς, Eph.1:17; 
  (d) of Christ (Dalman, Words, 190 ff.); 
  (α) by our Lord himself: ὁ π., Matt.11:25-27, Luke.10:2, 22 John.5:20-23, al.; ὁ π. μου, Matt.11:27, al.; ὁ ἐν τ. οὐρανοῖς, Matt.7:11, al.; ὁ οὐράνιος, Matt.15:13; vocat., John.11:41 12:27, 28 17:1, 5, 11, 20, 25 (cf. Abbott, JG., 96 f.); 
  (β) by Apostles: John.1:14 (anarth.; see M, Pr., l.with), Rom.15:6, 2 Cor.1:3 11:31, Eph.1:3, Col.1:3, Heb.1:5, 1 Pet.1:3, Rev.1:6 (cf. Westc., Epp. Jo., 27-34).
 (AS)

G3985.d:
πειράζω  poët. and late prose form of πειράω, which see, 
 [in LXX for נָסָה pi. ;] 
  1. to make proof of (Hom.). 
  2. to try, attempt (Luc., Polyb., with inf. (see M, Pr., 205; B1., § 69, 4), Acts.9:26 16:7 24:6. 
  3. In LXX and NT, like Heb. נָסָה, with accusative of person(s), to test, try, prove; 
  (a) in a good sense : John.6:6, 2 Cor.13:5, Heb.11:17, Rev.2:2; esp. of trials and af6ictions sent or permitted by God (Gen.22:1, Exod.20:20, Wis.3:5, al.), 1 Cor.10:13, Heb.2:18 4:15 11:17, 37, Rev.3:10; 
  (b) in a bad sense (Apoll. Rhod., 3, 10): of the attempts made to ensnare Jesus in his speech, Matt.16:1 19:3 22:18, 35 Mark.8:11 10:2 12:15, Luke.11:16, John.8:6; of temptation to sin, to tempt, Jas.1:13-14 (see Hort, in l), Gal.6:1, Rev.2:10; esp. of temptations of the devil, Matt.4:1, 3 Mark.1:13, Luke.4:2, 1 Cor.7:5, 1 Thess.3:5; ὁ πειράζων, the tempter, Matt.4:3, 1 Thess.3:5; 
  (with) in bad sense also (Exod.17:2, 7, Num.14:22, al.), of distrustful testing, trying or challenging of God: Acts.15:10, 1 Cor.10:9 (WH, mg., ἐξεπείρασαν), Heb.3:9; τ. πνεῦμα Κυρίου, Acts.5:9 (of. ἐκ-πειράζω).†
 SYN.: δοκιμάζω, q.v  (AS)

G3992.d:
πέμπω 
 [in LXX chiefly for שָׁלַח ;] 
to send; 
  (a) of persons: with accusative, absol., Matt.22:7, Luke.7:19, John.1:22, 2 Cor.9:3, al.; ptcp. before verb., Matt.14:10, Acts.19:31, al.; ptcp. before διά (= Heb. יָד;, 1Ki.16:20, al.), Matt.11:2 (cf. Rev.1:1); of teachers sent by God, John.1:33 4:34, Rom.8:3, al.; with accusative and dative, 1 Cor.4:17, Phil.2:19; before πρός, with accusative, Luke.4:26, John.16:7, al.; before λέγων (cf. Heb. אָמַר שָׁלַח Gen.38:25, al.), Luke.7:6, 19; before εἰς, with accusative loc., Matt.2:8, Luke.15:15, al.; before εἰς (of purpose), Eph.6:22, Col.4:8, 1 Pet.2:14; with inf., John.1:33, 1 Cor.16:3, Rev.22:16; 
  (b) of things : Rev.11:10; before εἰς, Rev.1:11; id., of purpose, Acts.11:29, Phil.4:16; with dative of person(s), 2 Thess.2:11; π. τ. δρέπανον σου (cf. εξαποστείλατε δρέπανα = מַגָּל שָׁלַח, Jol.3:13), Rev.14:15, 18 (cf. ἀνα-, ἐκ-, μετα-, προ-, συν-πέμπω) 
 SYN.: ἀποστέλλω, q.v  (AS)

